From daecfd2d533e5e86eb20c05928209a9e6aa5bd65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 12:56:31 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] d/wmr: Survive transient companion device drops during
 startup

The HP Reverb G2's companion HID device (03f0:0580) can drop off the USB
bus and re-enumerate while the headset display powers up: the headset's
internal USB 2.0 hub (04b4:6506) resets under panel load, taking the
companion and the audio function down with it. Root cause is still under
investigation (the same unit runs for hours under Windows 11 without a
single drop, so it is not simply failing hardware) - but whatever the
cause, a transient companion hiccup should not kill the session.

Two consequences inside the driver today:

1. control_read_packets() treated any os_hid_read error as fatal and
   returned false, which breaks the whole wmr_run_thread read loop -
   killing the hololens sensors feed (IMU and tunnelled controller
   packets, a separate and typically still-healthy device) over an
   unrelated companion hiccup. Log at debug level and keep the thread
   alive instead. No extra pacing is needed: the loop is already paced
   by the blocking hololens read.

2. The screen-enable command sent during wmr_hmd_activate_reverb() can
   be silently lost if the companion device drops right around
   activation (HID_SEND only logs on failure). Re-send it after the
   settle sleep; the command is idempotent. This also runs on the G1,
   which shares the activation function; the resend is a no-op when the
   first command landed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
---
 src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c
index 0e7da4619..b617e13fa 100644
--- a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c
+++ b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c
@@ -586,9 +586,15 @@ control_read_packets(struct wmr_hmd *wh)
 	os_mutex_unlock(&wh->hid_lock);
 
 	if (size < 0) {
-		WMR_ERROR(wh, "Error reading from companion (HMD control) device. Call to os_hid_read returned %i",
+		// The companion device can transiently drop off and re-enumerate while the
+		// headset display is powering up. Don't kill the whole read thread over a
+		// read error here: the same thread also reads the hololens sensors device
+		// (IMU and tunnelled controller packets), which is typically still healthy.
+		// No extra pacing: a failing read is one cheap syscall, and each loop
+		// iteration is already paced by the blocking hololens read.
+		WMR_DEBUG(wh, "Error reading from companion (HMD control) device. Call to os_hid_read returned %i",
 		          size);
-		return false;
+		return true;
 	}
 	if (size == 0) {
 		WMR_TRACE(wh, "No more data to read");
@@ -779,6 +785,11 @@ wmr_hmd_activate_reverb(struct wmr_hmd *wh)
 	uint64_t seconds = debug_get_num_option_sleep_seconds();
 	os_nanosleep(U_TIME_1S_IN_NS * seconds);
 
+	// The companion HID device can transiently drop off and re-enumerate right around
+	// activation, which can silently lose the screen-enable command sent above. Resend it
+	// now that things have had time to settle, so the panel doesn't stay stuck off/blank.
+	wmr_hmd_screen_enable_reverb(wh, wh->hmd_screen_enable);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
